> residue 1/(z^2+1) at i
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/(z^2 + 1) at z = i:
  = -i/2
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/(z^2 + 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue 1/(z^2+1) at -i
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/(z^2 + 1) at z = -i:
  = i/2
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/(z^2 + 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue 1/z^3 at 0
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/z^3 at z = 0:
  = 0
  a pole of order 3 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-3)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/z^3 on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue sin(z)/z^2 at 0
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of sin(z)/z^2 at z = 0:
  = 1
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of sin(z)/z^2 on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue exp(z)/z at 0
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of exp(z)/z at z = 0:
  = 1
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of exp(z)/z on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue 1/(z^2-1) at 1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/(z^2 - 1) at z = 1:
  = 1/2
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/(z^2 - 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue z^2/(z-1)^3 at 1
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of z^2/(z - 1)^3 at z = 1:
  = 1
  a pole of order 3 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-3)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of z^2/(z - 1)^3 on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue 1/sin(z) at 0
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/sin(z) at z = 0:
  = 1
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/sin(z) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
> residue exp(1/z) at 0
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of exp(1/z) at z = 0:
  the integral around small circles does not settle (an essential singularity or a branch point)
> taylor abs(x) x at 0 order 5
no Taylor series of abs(x) at x = 0: at x = 0 abs(x) has a corner (slope -1 from the left, 1 from the right)
> taylor exp(-1/x^2) x at 0 order 6
no Taylor series of exp(-1/x^2) at x = 0: it is undefined there
> taylor sqrt(x) x at 0 order 4
no Taylor series of sqrt(x) at x = 0: at x = 0 (an end of the domain) sqrt(x) has an infinite one-sided slope
> taylor log(x) x at 0 order 4
no Taylor series of log(x) at x = 0: it is undefined there
> taylor sign(x) x at 0 order 4
no Taylor series of sign(x) at x = 0: at x = 0 sign(x) jumps (not continuous)
